course: history
lesson: 75
title: Ideas on the move: numbers, paper, letters, chess and news
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Ideas travel like a football passed down a long line of players: the digits you write in maths came from India, were passed on by scholars writing in Arabic, and reached Europe through a merchant's son from Pisa, Fibonacci, in 1202.
explain: In Europa Universalis «institutions» like the printing press spread from province to province over a few game decades; in real life ideas often took centuries, and sometimes people pushed back, as when money-changers in Florence banned the new numbers in 1299.
explain: The letter A began as a little drawing of an ox's head, paper crept from China to Spain over a thousand years, chess was born in India, and a Cinderella story was written down in China around 850, long before any cartoon.
explain: News used to travel at the speed of a horse or a ship; now it flies round the world in seconds, true and false alike, and studies suggest false stories can spread even faster than true ones.
explain: So a good habit is old and new at once: ask where an idea came from, and check before you share.
fact: Our digits came from India, spread through scholars writing in Arabic such as al-Khwarizmi around 825, and reached Europe through writers such as Fibonacci in 1202.
fact: In 1299 the money-changers' guild of Florence banned the new digits in account books, fearing they were easy to alter; good ideas can take centuries to win.
fact: Paper was made in China by about 100 CE; it reached Samarkand around 751, Baghdad around 794, Spain in the 1100s and later the rest of Europe.
fact: The alphabet began with signs made by workers in Egypt and Sinai around 1800 BCE; the first letter, which became A, was a picture of an ox's head.
fact: The Phoenicians spread their alphabet around the Mediterranean from about 1050 BCE; the Greeks added vowels, and the Latin and Cyrillic alphabets grew from the Greek one.
fact: Between about 800 and 200 BCE great thinkers lived in China, India, Persia, Israel and Greece, among them Confucius, the Buddha and Socrates; they did not know each other, and some historians call this the Axial Age while others say the pattern is too neat.
fact: News of the Battle of Waterloo in 1815 took days to reach London, and news of Lincoln's death in 1865 took about 12 days to reach Europe; after the Atlantic telegraph cable of 1866 news crossed in minutes.
fact: Chess began in India around 600 CE and spread to Persia, the Arab world and Europe; the queen became the strongest piece only around 1475, in Spain.
fact: A Chinese Cinderella story, Ye Xian, was written down around 850 CE, and similar stories are told in many cultures.
fact: About 2.3 billion people are Christians, about 2 billion Muslims and about 1.2 billion Hindus, and about 1.9 billion people have no religion, according to a large 2020 count.
fact: About 2,000 years ago Hero of Alexandria made a spinning steam toy, but nobody used steam to do work until the first steam engines around 1700; a working engine needed strong metal parts, plenty of fuel like coal and a real job to do, such as pumping water out of mines.
fact: A large 2018 study of social media found that false news spread faster and further than true news.
fact: In the 1100s scholars in Toledo, in Spain, translated Arabic books into Latin, bringing Greek and Arab science to European readers.
exercise: Where did our digits, 0 to 9, first come from? | India | Ancient Rome | England | Egypt, on the Nile
exercise: Who helped bring the digits to Europe in 1202? | Fibonacci | Julius Caesar | Confucius | Hero of Alexandria
exercise: Why did Florence's money-changers ban the new digits in 1299? | They feared the digits were easy to alter | They thought the digits were too beautiful | The Pope invented them | They had no use for numbers
exercise: Where was paper first made? | China | Moorish Spain | Greece | England
exercise: Roughly when did paper reach Spain? | In the 1100s | Around 100 CE | In 1900 | Before the pyramids
exercise: What was the letter A first a picture of? | An ox's head | A tall mountain | A tent in the desert | A fish
exercise: What did the Greeks add to the alphabet? | Vowels | Numbers | Commas | Capital letters only
exercise: Did Confucius, the Buddha and Socrates know each other? | No, they lived far apart and never met | Yes, they wrote long letters to each other every week | Yes, they studied together at the same school | Yes, they were brothers
exercise: How long did news of Lincoln's death take to reach Europe in 1865? | About 12 days | A few seconds | About 12 years | About 12 minutes
exercise: What made news cross the Atlantic in minutes after 1866? | A telegraph cable under the ocean | Much faster sailing ships | Trained carrier pigeons | Radio towers built on islands across the sea
exercise: Where did chess begin? | India | France | England | Russia
exercise: When did the queen become the strongest chess piece? | Around 1475, in Spain | At the very start in India | In 1990 | It never did
exercise: How old is a Chinese Cinderella story? | Written down around 850 CE | Made by a film studio in 1950 | Written in 2010 | It does not exist
exercise: Which religion has the most followers in the world? | Christianity | Buddhism, in Asia | Sikhism | Judaism
exercise: Why didn't Hero's steam toy lead to steam trains? | Nobody used it for work; it stayed a curiosity | Steam had not been discovered yet in his time | The Romans banned all toys in the year 50 | It ran on electricity, which they could not make
exercise: What did a 2018 study find about false news online? | It spread faster and further than true news | It always disappears within a minute of posting | It never gets shared by real people | It is always marked as false
exercise: What happened in Toledo in the 1100s? | Scholars translated Arabic books into Latin | Paper was invented there by a monk | Chess was invented by the king of Toledo | The first newspaper in Europe was printed
test: Ali: "Our numbers came from India via the Arabs." His mate from Florence: "Fun fact, Florence literally banned 'em!" Who's right? | Both: the digits came from India via Arabic scholars, and Florence banned them in 1299 | Only Ali; nobody ever banned digits anywhere, because they were always seen as useful | Only the mate; digits came from Florence and spread to India | Neither; digits came from Rome
test: In Europa Universalis the printing press spreads from province to province in a few decades. What was paper's real journey from China to Spain? | About a thousand years, step by step | A few weeks, carried by fast horses | One day, by order of the Spanish king | It never reached Spain at all
test: Zara: "A was an ox head? No way, that's mad!" Her nan: "Aye, pet, letters were pictures once." ("Pet" means dear, in the North East.) What's true? | The first letter began as a drawing of an ox's head | Letters were invented by the Romans from nothing at all, in one go | A was first a picture of an apple | Letters were always just lines
test: Ethan: "Confucius, Buddha and Socrates lived at the same time? Did they, like, have a group chat?" Best answer? | No, they lived far apart and never knew of each other | Yes, sort of: they sent notes by carrier pigeon every week | Yes, they met up in Athens once a year | They were all the same person
test: Ollie's Gen X dad: "Back in my day, news took ages, not like your phones." How long did news of Waterloo take to reach London in 1815? | Days | Seconds | Years | It never arrived
test: Kai's cousin: "Chess is proper English, innit, knights and castles." What's the correction? | Chess began in India and spread through Persia and the Arab world | Your cousin's right, chess was invented in London by English knights | Chess began in Ancient Rome | Chess came from America
test: Ella's friend: "Cinderella's Disney, end of." Ella: "Nah, there's a Chinese one from ages ago." Who's right? | Ella: a Chinese version was written down around 850 CE | The friend: Disney invented the whole story in 1950 | Neither: Cinderella is from 2000 | Both: Disney lived in China
test: Aisha asks which religions have the most people. Her mate says, "Islam is, like, mid-size." ("Mid" here means middling.) What does a big 2020 count say? | Christianity first, Islam second, with about 2 billion followers | Islam is one of the smallest religions, with only a few million followers | Buddhism has 5 billion followers | Nobody follows any religion now
test: Tom: "The Greeks had a steam toy and didn't make trains? Big L." ("L" means loss.) What would they have needed? | A use for steam power, metal skills and fuel all together | Only a bigger toy and a few more slaves to turn it | Nothing, because trains need no steam | Permission from the Romans
test: Lily's bro shares a fake video: "It's got a million views, it's legit." ("Legit" means real.) What's the wise reply? | Lots of shares does not make it true; check before sharing | A million views proves it's true, so share it with everyone | Fake videos cannot be shared | Videos online are always checked
test: Aarav: "Scholars in Toledo translating books? Like Google Translate, but slow?" What did they actually do? | Translated Arabic books into Latin, bringing science to Europe | Translated English books into Chinese | Built the first computer | Burned all foreign books
test: What would have happened if paper had never left China? | Books in other lands would have stayed costly, written on skins | Everyone would have started using phones much earlier instead | Europe would have had even more books | Nothing at all would have changed
exam: Identify the region from which the digits now employed in arithmetic originated. | India | Ancient Rome | England | Ancient Egypt
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the dissemination of paper from China? | It proceeded gradually, reaching Spain in the twelfth century | It occurred within a single year by decree of the Chinese emperor | Paper was invented independently in Spain before China | Paper never left the territory of China
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of the game of chess. | It originated in India and was transmitted through Persia and the Arab world | It was devised in England during the twentieth century | It originated in Ancient Rome | It was devised by Fibonacci in twelve hundred and two
exam: Ascertain the principal finding of a study published in two thousand and eighteen concerning false news upon social media. | False news was disseminated faster and further than true news | False news was never disseminated | True news was disseminated a hundred times faster | False news was invariably labelled as such
